// <memory> - [memory]: the allocator and its traits, the uninitialized algorithms, and the owning
// pointers of [unique.ptr] and [util.smartptr.shared].
//
// **unique_ptr<T, D>** owns one object, or an array for `unique_ptr<T[], D>`, and hands it to its
// deleter; an empty deleter takes no room. **shared_ptr<T>** counts its owners in a control block
// that also holds the deleter, and a weak_ptr counts in it as well, so the block outlives the
// object while anything watches it. The counts are plain integers: there are no threads here
// (<thread> and <atomic> are absent), so they are not atomic, and the atomic_ functions over a
// shared_ptr are the plain operations.
//
// **What does not work yet, and why.** make_shared, allocate_shared, the three pointer casts and
// get_deleter are written but cannot be called: each names its first template argument and
// deduces the rest, which this compiler does not do yet; nor can allocator_traits' construct and
// destroy, static member templates of a class template. allocator_traits and pointer_traits have
// no rebind (a member alias template of a class template), and a converting assignment is a
// conversion and then a move (a member operator= template is not taken yet). The places where a
// constructor departs from [util.smartptr] for the same reasons say so where they are.
#ifndef _CXX1_MEMORY
#define _CXX1_MEMORY

#include <cstddef>
#include <exception>
#include <new>
#include <typeinfo>
#include <utility>
#include <__allocator>
#include <__iterator_base>

